    "case_id": "497",
    "case_summary": "A 58-year-old woman presented after developing right arm weakness 8 hours into a long-haul flight. Medical history includes Coombs-positive autoimmune haemolytic anaemia. The patient was dehydrated at presentation.\n\nRight arm weakness was noted. Other physical examination details were not explicitly mentioned in the case report.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['- Blood film showed auto-agglutination with macro-aggregates of red cells up to 100 μm in length and rouleaux', '- Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: 119 mm/h (normal <10 mm/h)', '- D-dimer: 24.7 nmol/L (<3.0 nmol/L)', '- Fibrinogen: 15.6 μmol/L (5.6–11.8 μmol/L)', '- Renal function showed signs of dehydration', '- Elevated tumor markers', '- Procoagulant and vasculitic studies were negative'], 'Cardiac Studies': ['- ECG and telemetry showed normal sinus rhythm', '- Transthoracic echocardiography findings:', '  * Normal cardiac chamber size and function', '  * Normal valve function', '  * Negative agitated saline contrast', '  * Multiple echogenic targets with snowflake appearance in all four heart chambers', '  * Rapid swirling motion detected on M-mode imaging'], 'Imaging Studies': ['- Brain MRI showed:', '  * Multiple small foci of restricted diffusion in the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral artery territories', '  * Widespread microhaemorrhages', '- Carotid ultrasound showed normal carotid and vertebral artery anatomy']}",
